        Plan for this region: A wide, terraced earthen excavation corridor running north-to-south, characterized by a central sunken dirt trench flanked by elevated western and eastern dirt terraces. The central trench features several gargantuan ancient brass clockwork gears, up to ten meters in diameter, half-buried in the soil at various angles to block central sightlines. Multiple weathering granite obelisks stand or lie shattered across the corridor, with one fallen obelisk bridging the central trench. The perimeter is reinforced by industrial steel mesh retaining walls and concrete shoring structures that hold back the earthen terraced slopes. Modern industrial excavation equipment, including metal conveyor ramps, pipeline segments on the ground, and clusters of concrete barricades, are distributed across the lane to provide cover. Wide gravel ramps at the northern and southern boundaries provide transition paths to the landing pad and temple camp, respectively, while the open eastern face connects directly to the cavern entrances.
