Subregion plan: An ascending subterranean passage running from north to south, bridging the lower central cavern junction to the southern temple camp cave mouth. The central zone of this region is defined by a wide, steep dirt and gravel slope carrying the continuation of the narrow-gauge mine tracks upward. Flanking this central slope on the eastern side is a multi-tier modular timber scaffolding structure that provides an elevated pedestrian bypass route with protective wooden railings. The western wall features deep, carved rock recesses utilized as storage alcoves for heavy mining equipment, secured with timber framing. Structural square-timber support arches span across the passage at regular intervals, increasing in density as the slope nears the southern exit. Thick industrial electrical cabling bundles are bracketed along these timber supports and the limestone ceiling, running the length of the slope to link the power grids between adjacent zones.



